Hi Nick,

Thanks for your answer ! OK, so the issue is more complicated than I
thought – I admit that I am not that deep into modelling to know how
to develop such a hierarchical model.

So for the moment, I'll just try several types of area classification
and compare them in terms of parameters and ancestral areas inferred,
knowing that I cannot compare them directly through likelihoods.

Jan

Quoting Nick Matzke <nickmat...@gmail.com>:

> Hi!  I am deep in revisions, but briefly: This is a deep question that I
> have also thought about.  Your statements are correct about changing the
> model vs. changing the data.  I feel the question needs some serious
> thinking and probably a paper.  My undeveloped thought is that perhaps the
> solution would be a hierarchical model:
>
> Level 1: raw observations from e.g. GBIF
> Level 2: areas classification
> Level 3: historical biogeography model
>
> Normally we just have Level 2 as data and Level 3 as model. But if Level 2
> could somehow be turned into a probabilistic model conferring a likelihood
> on Level 1, just as Level 3 confers a likelihood on Level 2, it might all
> work.
>
> Further thoughts/discussion welcome!
>
> Nick
